# The reverse proxy in front of the web app (CS-119, docs/runbooks/deploy.md): the only thing the server publishes (ports 80
# and 443). It speaks HTTPS to visitors and plain http to the web app on the compose network, where Next.js listens
# and nothing else can reach it.
#
# CARSHENAS_TLS_MODE (in .env) picks how the certificate comes:
#   auto      a domain you own, pointing at this server: Caddy asks Let's Encrypt for a certificate and renews it
#             itself (needs ports 80 and 443 reachable from the internet, and ACME_EMAIL)
#   manual    a domain, and a certificate you bring: certs/fullchain.pem and certs/privkey.pem
#   internal  no domain, only the server's address: Caddy's own certificate authority signs one; browsers warn once
#   acme-ip   no domain, only the server's address, with a six-day Let's Encrypt certificate for the address (experimental)
#   cdn       a CDN (ArvanCloud) answers visitors over HTTPS with its own certificate and forwards plain http to port 80:
#             no certificate here; the visitor's address comes from the CDN's X-Forwarded-For, trusted only from the
#             ranges in CARSHENAS_TRUSTED_PROXIES
# CARSHENAS_UNLISTED (in .env, 1 or 0) picks the visibility: conf.d/visibility-1.caddy (noindex on every answer, also the
# ones this proxy writes itself) or visibility-0.caddy (nothing added). The web app writes the same header for itself.
{
	# Plain h1 and h2: the UDP port of HTTP/3 is not published, and a network may throttle it.
	servers {
		protocols h1 h2
		# Whose X-Forwarded-For is believed: nobody but loopback, unless the cdn mode lists the CDN's edge ranges. Strict:
		# the address is read right to left, skipping trusted hops, so a visitor cannot plant one.
		trusted_proxies static {$CARSHENAS_TRUSTED_PROXIES:127.0.0.1/32}
		trusted_proxies_strict
		client_ip_headers X-Forwarded-For
	}
}

import conf.d/common.caddy
import conf.d/mode-{$CARSHENAS_TLS_MODE:auto}.caddy
